  The border-only take: no yellow wash, the ring held while a field is typed
  into, and square elements rounded while lit.

EVERY SCENE HAS A LEAD-IN AND AN EXIT PAUSE.

  lead-in  0.5s   Sarah is on screen before she starts. A scene that opens
                mid-word has no edit point, and cuts hard against the one
                before it.
  exit     0.8s   after she stops. This is the join: it is what the next
                scene's crossfade eats into, and what an editor trims to.

  So a scene is  lead-in + speech + exit, and the cue below covers ONLY the
  speech. The scene's own start and end are in its NOTE.

THE EXIT PAUSE IS DECLARED, NOT YET AUTOMATIC. The pipeline's `pauses` field
(script.json, one entry per scene here) is summed into the spoken length, but
build/preview_narration.py's own docstring says the splice is NOT performed by
assemble_video.py — scene 7 of the first-time-ordering video was spliced by
hand. Treat the exit as a number to cut to, not one that appears on its own.

SPEAKING RATE 3.44 words a second. MEASURED, from two rendered clips —
31 words in 9.01s — see script_v33.json's _wps_note. An earlier draft of this
file used 2.58 and every scene came out about a third too long.

Still an ESTIMATE. Nothing has been rendered for THIS video, so no line has a
real spoken length. Real ones on the first-time-ordering video ran between
2.8 and 3.9 words a second. Re-time from the audio once it exists.

